# 105 DECLARES NOTHING AT ALL, and it is phase 85's kind rather than phase 92's: the code
# runs, and the instrument can barely see it.  Seven wrapper functions that took `...`,
# opened a `va_list` and handed it to `vim_vsnprintf` are expanded at their 129 call
# sites into `vim_snprintf(...)` plus the tail the wrapper ran afterwards -- `msg()`,
# `msg_attr()`, `msg_attr_keep()`, `emsg()`, `iemsg()`, all five of which already
# existed and were already called from elsewhere, so no message logic is written here at
# all.  `va_start` goes from eight functions to one.  Two full recordings either side
# are byte-identical: 102 screen cases, ref-excmds.txt, ref-argv.txt, ref-pty.txt and
# ref-term.txt.
#
# HOW BLIND THE RECORDING IS, MEASURED rather than asserted.  The input source built
# again with `write(2, "ZW|<wrapper>|<format>\n", ...)` at the entry to each of the
# seven, run over the 102 screen cases: `vim_snprintf_safelen` is entered 617 times,
# `smsg_attr_keep` 6, `vim_snprintf_add` 2, and `smsg`, `smsg_attr`, `semsg` and
# `siemsg` NOT ONCE.  `semsg` is 94 of the 129 sites and the screen corpus never enters
# it.  So the phase owes probes, and phase/105/check.sh runs 263 of them on both
# binaries -- the Ex-command errors, 132 regexp errors over both engines and three magic
# settings (which are where the 18 comma-expression sites live), the report messages,
# the substitute-confirm prompt, undo, CTRL-G and the ruler, and four incsearch probes
# with a bad pattern, which are the only way to reach a `semsg` under `emsg_off > 0`.
# The same instrument says the probes enter `semsg` 232 times over 34 distinct formats
# and reach 67 of the 129 sites -- 66 found by mapping each runtime format string back
# through the `static char e_*[]` table to the site's format expression, plus
# `vim_snprintf_add`'s single site, whose format is a parameter and which the instrument
# shows entered twice.  The other 62 are covered by the edit being ONE RULE applied
# uniformly, and by the whole-file equalities the check makes: `semsg`'s unreached sites
# are out-of-memory reports, the twenty inside the formatter itself -- which fire only
# on a format string the editor would have to have got wrong, and every format in this
# file is one of its own -- and `siemsg`'s ten internal errors, which is the memfile
# detecting its own corruption.
#
# THE PROBES ARE PROVEN ABLE TO FAIL, WITH FOUR CONTROLS, AND TWO OF THEM MOVE NOTHING
# ON PURPOSE.  This phase's own output with both room helpers returning 20 instead of
# IOSIZE moves 129 of 263; with every `semsg` site given `msg()` for a tail instead of
# `emsg()` it moves 155.  With `safelen_result`'s clamp removed it moves 0, and with all
# three guards removed it moves 0 -- and those two are reported rather than dropped,
# because they are the honest statement of what the evidence cannot reach: the clamp
# needs a message longer than 1,025 bytes out of `fileinfo`, and the guards need
# `IObuff == NULL`, which is an out-of-memory failure of the first two allocations the
# process makes.
#
# NOTHING MOVES IN `nm -u` EITHER, and that is the other half of the same fact.  A pure
# restructure inside one translation unit frees nothing: `vim_vsnprintf_typval` still
# does every conversion in the same file, and `<stdarg.h>`'s three names are macros and
# a compiler builtin type, which are no symbol at all.  What the phase moves is not code
# across a boundary but the POSSIBILITY of drawing one.
